{"abstract":"Constructive type theory combines logic and programming in one language. This is useful both for reasoning about programs written in type theory, as well as for reasoning about other programming languages inside type theory. It is well-known that it is challenging to extend these applications to languages with recursion and computational effects such as probabilistic choice, because these features are not easily represented in constructive type theory. We show how to define and reason about FPC⊕, a programming language with probabilistic choice and recursive types, in guarded type theory. We use higher inductive types to represent finite distributions and guarded recursion to model recursion. We define both operational and denotational semantics of FPC⊕, as well as a relation between the two. The relation can be used to prove adequacy, but we also show how to use it to reason about programs up to contextual equivalence.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Stassen, Philipp Jan Andries","Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","Zwart, Maaike Annebet","Aguirre, Alejandro","Birkedal, Lars"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-797161"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers"],"keyword":["Guarded Recursion","Probabilistic Programming Languages","Recursive Types","Type Theory"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"modelling recursion and probabilistic choice in guarded type theory","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3704884","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-797161","identifier":[{"id":"85215684518","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-d5998eec-e914-4bfa-891b-ff8506bad04a"],"keyword":["Guarded Recursion","Probabilistic Programming Languages","Recursive Types","Type Theory"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-4-6-E5F8B8880E7E3B08E76C857F229FD589"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/3704884","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Philipp","key":"stassenpau","last":"Stassen","locid":"dcf5bf21-0adf-4a0e-bde4-c2044fe073e5","name":"Stassen, Philipp Jan Andries","name_org":"Stassen, Philipp","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus Ejlers","key":"møgelbergpau","last":"Møgelberg","name":"Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Maaike Annebet","key":"zwartpau","last":"Zwart","name":"Zwart, Maaike Annebet","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Alejandro","key":"aguirrepau","last":"Aguirre","locid":"ed9d6578-8666-4e42-988a-b91f6cf38b58","name":"Aguirre, Alejandro","orcid":"0000-0001-6746-2734","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars","key":"birkedalpau","last":"Birkedal","locid":"bd012524-7d01-45f4-9bbd-8f8273bf14c7","name":"Birkedal, Lars","orcid":"0000-0003-1320-0098","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/3704884","issn":["24751421"],"issue":"POPL","journal":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","pages":"1417-1445","paperid":"48","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85215684518"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-d5998eec-e914-4bfa-891b-ff8506bad04a"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Modelling Recursion and Probabilistic Choice in Guarded Type Theory","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-797161","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"Constructive type theory combines logic and programming in one language. This is useful both for reasoning about programs written in type theory, as well as for reasoning about other programming languages inside type theory. It is well-known that it is challenging to extend these applications to languages with recursion and computational effects such as probabilistic choice, because these features are not easily represented in constructive type theory. We show how to define and reason about a programming language with probabilistic choice and recursive types, in guarded type theory. We use higher inductive types to represent finite distributions and guarded recursion to model recursion. We define both operational and denotational semantics, as well as a relation between the two. The relation can be used to prove adequacy, but we also show how to use it to reason about programs up to contextual equivalence.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Stassen, Philipp","Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","Zwart, Maaike Annebet","Aguirre, Alejandro","Birkedal, Lars"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798554"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Guarded Recursion","Probabilistic Programming Languages","Recursive Types","Type Theory"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"modelling recursion and probabilistic choice in guarded type theory","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/370488","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-798554","identifier":[{"id":"85215684518","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-b6ae2ca3-8ed8-4b1c-b7ac-53993a159118"],"keyword":["Guarded Recursion","Probabilistic Programming Languages","Recursive Types","Type Theory"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-4-6-E5F8B8880E7E3B08E76C857F229FD589"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.04455","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1145/370488","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/108679372/popl2025-preprint.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Philipp","key":"stassenpau","last":"Stassen","name":"Stassen, Philipp","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus Ejlers","key":"møgelbergpau","last":"Møgelberg","locid":"10003d22-a201-4b64-88e3-b423bc76fcc2","name":"Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","orcid":"0000-0003-0386-4376","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Maaike Annebet","key":"zwartpau","last":"Zwart","locid":"4634ce05-3fcb-49ca-89c7-080955f450e8","name":"Zwart, Maaike Annebet","orcid":"0000-0002-0257-1574","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Alejandro","key":"aguirrepau","last":"Aguirre","name":"Aguirre, Alejandro","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars","key":"birkedalpau","last":"Birkedal","name":"Birkedal, Lars","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1145/370488","issn":["24751421"],"issue":"POPL","journal":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","publisher":"Association for Computing Machinery","status":"p","title":"Proceedings of the ACM on Programming Languages","volume":"9"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85215684518"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-b6ae2ca3-8ed8-4b1c-b7ac-53993a159118"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Modelling Recursion and Probabilistic Choice in Guarded Type Theory","type":"Conference Paper","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-798554","year":"2025","yearsub":"2025"}
{"abstract":"The delay monad provides a way to introduce general recursion in type theory. To write programs that use a wide range of computational effects directly in type theory, we need to combine the delay monad with the monads of these effects. Here we present a first systematic study of such combinations. We study both the coinductive delay monad and its guarded recursive cousin, giving concrete examples of combining these with well-known computational effects. We also provide general theorems stating which algebraic effects distribute over the delay monad, and which do not. Lastly, we salvage some of the impossible cases by considering distributive laws up to weak bisimilarity.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","Zwart, Maaike Annebet"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-798533"],"filters":{"added":"2025-04","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Guarded Recursion","Type Theory"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"what monads can and cannot do with a bit of extra time","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dcp","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CSL.2024.39","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-798533","ids":["itu-5ffdfce1-f80f-404a-a106-0c9edc674b53"],"keyword":["Distributive Laws","Guarded Recursion","Type Theory"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-05C55FEE66908814305C2ABF971BDFCE"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://doi.org/10.4230/LIPIcs.CSL.2024.39","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.15919v1","version":"Authors Final Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"I