{"abstract":"This article introduces the enriched effect calculus, which extends established type theories for computational effects with primitives from linear logic. The new calculus provides a formalism for expressing linear aspects of computational effects; e.g. the linear usage of imperative features such as state and/or continuations. The enriched effect calculus is implemented as an extension of a basic effect calculus without linear primitives, which is closely related to Moggi's computational metalanguage, Filinski's effect PCF and Levy's call-by-push-value. We present syntactic results showing: the fidelity of the behaviour of the linear connectives of the enriched effect calculus; the conservativity of the enriched effect calculus over its non-linear core (the effect calculus); and the non-conservativity of intuitionistic linear logic when considered as an extension of the enriched effect calculus. The second half of the article investigates models for the enriched effect calculus, based on enriched category theory. We give several examples of such models, relating them to models of standard effect calculi (such as those based on monads), and to models of intuitionistic linear logic. We also prove soundness and completeness.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","Simpson, Alex","Egger, Jeff"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382531"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Edinburgh"],"keyword":["Computational effects","Linear Logic"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the enriched eﬀect calculus syntax and semantics","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/logcom/exs025","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382531","identifier":[{"id":"84901462293","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-546f6f33-2233-4826-9340-8ea23a1abe6c"],"keyword":["Computational effects","Linear logic"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-BFCD7CC8A48D3E76DAFE109B159FDE47"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1093/logcom/exs025","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics","The Department, Theoretical Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of edinburgh","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Edinburgh","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus Ejlers","key":"møgelbergpau","last":"Møgelberg","locid":"10003d22-a201-4b64-88e3-b423bc76fcc2","name":"Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","orcid":"0000-0003-0386-4376","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Alex","key":"simpsonpau","last":"Simpson","name":"Simpson, Alex","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeff","key":"eggerpau","last":"Egger","name":"Egger, Jeff","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1093/logcom/exs025","issn":["0955792X","1465363X"],"issn_org":["0955792X"],"issue":"3","journal":"Journal of Logic and Computation","pages":"615-654","publisher":"Oxford University Press","status":"p","title":"Journal of Logic and Computation","volume":"24"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84901462293"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-546f6f33-2233-4826-9340-8ea23a1abe6c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"The Enriched Eﬀect Calculus: Syntax and Semantics","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382531","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"We investigate the phenomenon that \"every monad is a linear state monad\". We do this by studying a fully-complete state-passing translation from an impure call-by-value language to a new linear type theory: the enriched call-by-value calculus. The results are not specific to store, but can be applied to any computational effect expressible using algebraic operations, even to effects that are not usually thought of as stateful. There is a bijective correspondence between generic effects in the source language and state access operations in the enriched call-by-value calculus. From the perspective of categorical models, the enriched call-by-value calculus suggests a refinement of the traditional Kleisli models of effectful call-by-value languages. The new models can be understood as enriched adjunctions.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","Staton, Sam"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382001"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Cambridge"],"keyword":["Computational effects","Linear Type Theory","enriched adjunctions","monad","state passing translation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"linear usage of state","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2168/LMCS-10(1:17)2014","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382001","identifier":[{"id":"84897052876","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-32413b30-15af-47bf-8d2b-0404f2125f43"],"keyword":["computational effects","enriched adjunctions","linear type theory","monad","state-passing translation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-3-83C44C381FED0D85E17C9518E1D3F1C4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.lmcs-online.org/ojs/viewarticle.php?id=1229&layout=abstract","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2168/LMCS-10(1:17)2014","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics","The Department, Theoretical Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of cambridge","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Cambridge","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus Ejlers","key":"møgelbergpau","last":"Møgelberg","locid":"10003d22-a201-4b64-88e3-b423bc76fcc2","name":"Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","orcid":"0000-0003-0386-4376","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sam","key":"statonpau","last":"Staton","name":"Staton, Sam","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.2168/lmcs-10(1:17)2014","issn":["18605974"],"issue":"1","journal":"Logical Methods in Computer Science","publisher":"International Federation for Computational Logic","status":"p","title":"Logical Methods in Computer Science","volume":"10"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84897052876"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-32413b30-15af-47bf-8d2b-0404f2125f43"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Linear usage of state","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382001","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Guarded recursive functions and types are useful for giving semantics to advanced programming languages and for higher-order programming with infinite data types, such as streams, e.g., for modeling reactive systems. We propose an extension of intensional type theory with rules for forming fixed points of guarded recursive functions. Guarded recursive types can be formed simply by taking fixed points of guarded recursive functions on the universe of types. Moreover, we present a general model construction for constructing models of the intensional type theory with guarded recursive functions and types. When applied to the groupoid model of intensional type theory with the universe of small discrete groupoids, the construction gives a model of guarded recursion for which there is a one-to-one correspondence between fixed points of functions on the universe of types and fixed points of (suitable) operators on types. In particular, we find that the functor category Grpdωop from the preordered set of natural numbers to the category of groupoids is a model of intensional type theory with guarded recursive types.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Birkedal, Lars","Mogelberg, R.E."],"cuid":["S02-084.44-030230"],"event":{"acronym":"LICS","end":"2014-01-28","place":"New Orleans, us","start":"2013-06-25","title":"Annual IEEE/ACM Symposium on Logic in Computer Science","year":"2013"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"keyword":["Computational modeling","Equations","Mathematical model","Productivity","Programming","Semantics","Syntactics"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"intensional type theory with guarded recursive types qua fixed points on universes","src":["au","itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LICS.2013.27","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-084.44-030230","identifier":[{"id":"84883316540","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-f2d03e46-0aa7-4840-acd7-6820c29bd101","itu-28772cd0-3568-41ff-905a-887b454434a2"],"keyword":["Computational modeling","Equations","Mathematical model","Productivity","Programming","Semantics","Syntactics"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B40A5EE4183AB384F8D9F9C9F1E2B5DA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1109/LICS.2013.27","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Blocked","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2013"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Natural Sciences, Department of Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics","The Department, Theoretical Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Lars","key":"birkedalpau","last":"Birkedal","locid":"bd012524-7d01-45f4-9bbd-8f8273bf14c7","name":"Birkedal, Lars","orcid":"0000-0003-1320-0098","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus Ejlers","key":"møgelbergpau","last":"Møgelberg","locid":"10003d22-a201-4b64-88e3-b423bc76fcc2","name":"Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","orcid":"0000-0003-0386-4376","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1109/lics.2013.27","issn":["10436871"],"journal":"Annual Symposium on Logic in Computer Science","pages":"213-222","publisher":"Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers Inc.","status":"p","title":"Annual Symposium on Logic in Computer Science"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84883316540"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-f2d03e46-0aa7-4840-acd7-6820c29bd101","itu-28772cd0-3568-41ff-905a-887b454434a2"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Intensional Type Theory with Guarded Recursive Types qua Fixed Points on Universes","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-084.44-030230","year":"2013","yearsub":"2013"}
{"abstract":"The enriched effect calculus (EEC) is an extension of Moggi's computational metalanguage with a selection of primitives from linear logic. This paper explores the enriched effect calculus as a target language for continuation-passing-style (CPS) translations in which the typing of the translations enforces the linear usage of continuations. We first observe that established call-by-value and call-by name linear-use CPS translations of simply-typed lambda-calculus into intuitionistic linear logic (ILL) land in the fragment of ILL given by EEC. These two translations are uniformly generalised by a single generic translation of the enriched effect calculus into itself. As our main theorem, we prove that the generic self-translation of EEC is involutive up to isomorphism. As corollaries, we obtain full completeness results, both for the generic translation, and for the original call-by-value and call-by-name translations.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","Simpson, Alex","Egger, Jeff"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383147"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Edinburgh"],"keyword":["Continuation passing style","Enriched Effect Calculus","Generic Translation","Involutive Transformation","Linear Logic"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"linear use cps translations in the enriched effect calculus","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/84868144683","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383147","identifier":[{"id":"84868144683","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-80608fb7-0d82-4e01-9f69-9926d3a48bbe"],"keyword":["Continuation-Passing Style","Enriched Effect Calculus","Generic Translation","Involutive Transformation","Linear Logic"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-294B822189F6DE3DE4E370A444284147"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Theoretical Computer Science, Programming Logic and Semantics","The Department, Theoretical Computer Science"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"university of edinburgh","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Edinburgh","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus Ejlers","key":"møgelbergpau","last":"Møgelberg","locid":"10003d22-a201-4b64-88e3-b423bc76fcc2","name":"Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","orcid":"0000-0003-0386-4376","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Alex","key":"simpsonpau","last":"Simpson","name":"Simpson, Alex","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jeff","key":"eggerpau","last":"Egger","name":"Egger, Jeff","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["18605974"],"issue":"4","journal":"Logical Methods in Computer Science","publisher":"International Federation for Computational Logic","status":"p","title":"Logical Methods in Computer Science","volume":"8"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84868144683"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-80608fb7-0d82-4e01-9f69-9926d3a48bbe"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Linear-use CPS translations in the enriched effect calculus","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-383147","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"We present the topos S of trees as a model of guarded recursion. We study the internal dependently-typed higher-order logic of S and show that S models two modal operators, on predicates and types, which serve as guards in recursive definitions of terms, predicates, and types. In particular, we show how to solve recursive type equations involving dependent types. We propose that the internal logic of S provides the right setting for the synthetic construction of abstract versions of step-indexed models of programming languages and program logics. As an example, we show how to construct a model of a programming language with higher-order store and recursive types entirely inside the internal logic of S. Moreover, we give an axiomatic categorical treatment of models of synthetic guarded domain theory and prove that, for any complete Heyting algebra A with a well-founded basis, the topos of sheaves over A forms a model of synthetic guarded domain theory, generalizing the results for S.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Birkedal, Lars","Møgelberg, Rasmus Ejlers","Schwinghammer, Jan","Støvring, Kristian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-383013"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Computer Science","Logic in Computer Science"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"first steps in synthetic guarded domain theory step indexing in the topos of trees","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.2168/LMCS-8(4:1)2012","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-383013","identifier":[{"id":"84868100931","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-766f3e77-511c-4704-a310-4b7c383069b7"],"keyword":["Computer Science","Logic in Computer Science"],"l